diagnosis_text:
A full inspection of the Kath5 topology finds no fault. All OSPF adjacencies (covering areas 0, 1, 2 — the last connected via an OSPF virtual link between r4 and r8 — as well as area 3 on the r1–r11 link) have been continuously in Full state since initial lab convergence roughly eight and a half minutes before this investigation, with neighbor uptimes of 8m33s to 8m40s and zero subsequent log entries indicating any state change. The EIGRP AS 100 domain (r11, r12, r13) likewise shows all routes Passive in the DUAL topology table; EIGRP neighbor relationships on r11 have been stable since 01:05:52 with no resets recorded in any device log. The route to PC12's network (120.0.0.0/8) is present and stable on both r11 (via EIGRP from r12, age 9m17s) and r1 (redistributed OSPF external from r11, age 8m25s); end-to-end reachability between PC0 and PC12 is confirmed at 0% packet loss and 1–3 ms latency. The only noteworthy log entry was a one-time "sendmsg in ospf_write failed – Network is unreachable" on r8's VLINK0 at 01:06:45, ten seconds before the virtual-link adjacency to r4 completed its negotiation at 01:06:55. This is a normal transient race: r8's intra-area route to r4's address (45.1.1.1) had not yet been installed when the first VL hello fired. It left no repeat and caused no lasting outage. The user's description of periodic OSPF adjacency flapping and latency spikes every one to two minutes is not supported by any evidence; the network is healthy and no corrective action is required.
=== END ===